```
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {      // start; keep going while; step
    std::cout << i << "\n";
}

int fuel = 100;
while (fuel > 0) {                   // repeat while true
    fuel -= 30;
}

std::string word = "turret";
for (char c : word) {                // every item in turn
    std::cout << c << " ";
}
```

- `i++` adds one; `i += 5` adds five.
- `break;` jumps out of the loop. `continue;` skips the rest of this round.
- A loop whose condition never becomes false runs forever. In a game, the main loop is meant to; anywhere else it's a bug.

Game engines run your code inside a loop: Unreal calls `Tick(DeltaTime)` every frame, which is a loop you don't write yourself.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    for (int i = 3; i >= 1; i--) std::cout << i << "\n";
    std::cout << "Go!\n";
    int vowels = 0;
    for (char c : std::string("turret")) if (c == 'u' || c == 'e') vowels++;
    std::cout << vowels << "\n";
    return 0;
}

exercises.solution:
#include <string>
#include <cctype>

int countVowels(std::string s)
{
    int count = 0;
    for (char c : s) {
        char l = std::tolower(c);
        if (l == 'a' || l == 'e' || l == 'i' || l == 'o' || l == 'u') count++;
    }
    return count;
}

